Every integer is odd when its last decimal digit is 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9. The code first converts the value to a positive `long`, which also makes the minimum `int` value safe to handle.

This avoids loops and works for negative numbers as well. It does use a little more code than the usual remainder check.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isOdd(int n) {
        long value = Math.abs((long) n);
        long lastDigit = value % 10;
        return lastDigit == 1 || lastDigit == 3 || lastDigit == 5
                || lastDigit == 7 || lastDigit == 9;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: O(1)
Space complexity: O(1)

The last binary bit tells whether a number is odd. An odd number always ends in 1, so `n & 1` is nonzero for odd values.

Dry run with `n = 13`: binary 13 ends in 1, and `13 & 1` produces 1. With `n = 12`, the result is 0.

This is a small bitwise check and does not change the input value.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isOdd(int n) {
        return (n & 1) != 0;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: O(1)
Space complexity: O(1)

An even number can be written as two times an integer. I use the nearest integer quotient from `Math.trunc`, then compare the original number with that even value. If they differ, the number is odd.

Dry run with `n = -3`: `Math.trunc(-3 / 2)` is -1, and `-1 * 2` is -2. Since -3 is not -2, the result is `true`.

```javascript
class Solution {
    isOdd(n) {
        const half = Math.trunc(n / 2);
        return n !== half * 2;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: O(1)
Space complexity: O(1)

A remainder of 1 or -1 after division by 2 means the number is odd. In C++, checking `n % 2 != 0` handles both positive and negative odd numbers.

For example, 7 gives remainder 1, while -7 gives remainder -1. Zero and every even number give remainder 0.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isOdd(int n) {
        return n % 2 != 0;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: O(1)
Space complexity: O(1)

